---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import CTASection from '../components/CTASection.astro';
---

<PageLayout
  title="About"
  description="Learn about Blogs That Sell and the direct-response philosophy behind content that ranks and converts."
>
  <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-8">
    Blogs That Sell exists because most content marketing advice is broken.
  </p>

  <h2>The Origin Story</h2>

  <p>
    After years of watching businesses pour resources into content that generated pageviews but not customers, a pattern became clear:
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>The businesses getting real results from content were doing something different.</strong>
  </p>

  <p>
    They weren't following the standard "create valuable content" playbook. They were applying direct-response copywriting principles to their blog posts. Every piece of content was designed to convert, not just inform.
  </p>

  <p>
    The problem? This approach was mostly hidden in expensive courses and high-ticket consulting engagements. The average freelancer or small business owner had no access to these strategies.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Blogs That Sell changes that.</strong>
  </p>

  <h2>The Philosophy</h2>

  <p>
    We believe in a few core principles:
  </p>

  <h3>1. Content Should Sell</h3>
  <p>
    Not in a sleazy way. But every piece of content should move readers closer to a purchase. "Brand awareness" is a vanity metric. Revenue is what matters.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Quality Over Quantity</h3>
  <p>
    One blog post that converts is worth more than 100 posts that don't. We'd rather you publish 12 killer posts a year than 52 mediocre ones.
  </p>

  <h3>3. SEO + Conversion = Magic</h3>
  <p>
    Most people treat SEO and conversion as separate disciplines. They're not. The same principles that make content persuasive also make it perform better in search.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Systems Beat Talent</h3>
  <p>
    You don't need to be a gifted writer. You need proven frameworks and templates. Follow the system, get results.
  </p>

  <h2>Who's Behind This</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>John Fawkes</strong> is a direct-response copywriter and digital marketer with over 10 years of hands-on experience helping businesses turn words into revenue.
  </p>

  <p>
    His copy has generated millions in closed sales across industries—from $97 ebooks to $2,500 coaching packages. He's written sales letters, email sequences, VSLs, webinars, and sales funnels that consistently convert.
  </p>

  <p>
    A few highlights from the trenches:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Created a sales letter with a 5% conversion rate in financial services</li>
    <li>Built a 7-email webinar sequence that filled registrations in under a week</li>
    <li>Achieved a 33.5% conversion rate on a training course offer with 40% additional profit from order bumps</li>
    <li>Tripled leads from PPC campaigns while cutting advertising costs</li>
    <li>Wrote front-end copy generating thousands of monthly product sales</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    John has also developed copywriting courses teaching entrepreneurs how to write their own sales copy and build automated client acquisition systems.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Blogs That Sell</strong> is his answer to a problem he kept seeing: smart business owners creating content that got traffic but not customers. The solution? Apply the same direct-response principles that work in sales letters to blog content. That's what this site teaches.

  <h2>The Mission</h2>

  <p>
    Our mission is simple: help freelancers, consultants, and small business owners turn their blogs into customer acquisition machines.
  </p>

  <p>
    Not through tricks or hacks. Through solid direct-response principles applied to modern SEO content.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you're tired of creating content that doesn't convert, you're in the right place.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose">
    <CTASection
      headline="Ready to Get Started?"
      subheadline="Start with the free training to learn the core framework."
      primaryCta={{ text: 'Get the Free Training', href: '/free-training' }}
      variant="accent"
    />
  </div>
</PageLayout>
